WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.673
Let’s start by talking about what the

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:02.654
print function does.

3
00:00:03.315 --> 00:00:05.617
The print function is a tool in Python

4
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:07.700
that displays output on the screen.

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.962
It's one of the first things you'll use

6
00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:11.584
when you start coding in Python.

7
00:00:12.024 --> 00:00:14.107
You can print text, numbers, and more.

8
00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:16.629
This function is essential for showing

9
00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.632
results and messages while you work on

10
00:00:18.652 --> 00:00:19.873
your programs.

11
00:00:19.893 --> 00:00:22.196
So, it’s really important to understand

12
00:00:22.256 --> 00:00:23.457
how it works.

13
00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:25.651
Using the print function is pretty

14
00:00:25.751 --> 00:00:26.452
straightforward.

15
00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:28.475
You call it by writing print with

16
00:00:28.556 --> 00:00:29.197
parentheses.

17
00:00:29.938 --> 00:00:32.362
Inside those parentheses, you put the text

18
00:00:32.382 --> 00:00:34.124
you want to display, and it should be

19
00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:35.326
enclosed in quotes.

20
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:37.269
For example, if you write print('Hello,

21
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.455
World!'), it will display 'Hello, World!'

22
00:00:40.895 --> 00:00:41.396
on the screen.

23
00:00:42.077 --> 00:00:43.139
Let’s see that in action.

24
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.042
Here’s what it looks like in code.

25
00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:47.305
When you run this, you’ll see the output

26
00:00:47.406 --> 00:00:48.087
right below it.

27
00:00:48.587 --> 00:00:50.350
It’s a simple way to make Python say

28
00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:51.452
something.

29
00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:53.903
You can also print multiple items at once.

30
00:00:54.504 --> 00:00:56.528
To do this, separate each item with a

31
00:00:56.569 --> 00:00:56.969
comma.

32
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.154
When you do this, they will be displayed

33
00:00:59.214 --> 00:01:00.336
with a space in between.

34
00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:02.220
For example, if you write print('Hello',

35
00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:05.266
'World!'), it will print 'Hello World!'.

36
00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:07.350
Let’s take a look at how that works

37
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.011
in practice.

38
00:01:08.632 --> 00:01:10.496
Here’s the code, and when you run it,

39
00:01:10.716 --> 00:01:12.280
you’ll see how the items are printed

40
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.542
together.

41
00:01:13.542 --> 00:01:15.384
The print function can also display

42
00:01:15.464 --> 00:01:15.845
numbers.

43
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:18.288
You can print the results of calculations

44
00:01:18.368 --> 00:01:18.869
directly.

45
00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:20.771
For instance, if you write

46
00:01:21.852 --> 00:01:23.735
print(5 + 3), it will display 8.

47
00:01:24.235 --> 00:01:25.637
This is a great way to see the

48
00:01:25.697 --> 00:01:27.800
results of your math operations right on

49
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:28.281
the screen.

50
00:01:28.961 --> 00:01:30.343
Let’s check that out with a quick

51
00:01:30.403 --> 00:01:30.824
example.

52
00:01:31.404 --> 00:01:33.207
Here’s the code you would use, and when

53
00:01:33.247 --> 00:01:35.850
you run it, you’ll see the output.

54
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.075
Now it’s your turn to experiment with

55
00:01:38.095 --> 00:01:38.816
the print function.

56
00:01:39.418 --> 00:01:41.182
Try printing different types of data.

57
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.506
You can mix text and numbers in your

58
